<p>Read that again, because it's the whole category in one sentence. The middle of the blanket — the part over your body, the part doing the actual work — quietly stops weighing anything. The heavy edges hang off the mattress and drag the blanket floorward all night. And because it happens gradually, almost nobody diagnoses it. They just conclude weighted blankets "stopped working" for them.</p>

<h3>Check yours right now — it takes ten seconds</h3>

<p>If there's a weighted blanket in your house, go lay it flat. Run your hand down the centre line, then along the ends. If the ends feel dense and the middle feels like two sheets of fabric, you're not imagining it — you're sleeping under the memory of a weighted blanket. That test is the entire argument of this page, and you just ran it yourself.</p>

