<p>Why do the beads move? Baffle size. Big quilted compartments — six, seven, eight inches across — give the fill room to travel every time the blanket is lifted, washed or slept under. Small pockets don't. This isn't our invention: it's the spec the market itself settled on. The category's best-selling budget brand has spent seven years shrinking its pockets — 8-inch squares in 2016, 5-inch in 2019, a patented 2-inch grid by 2023 — precisely because buyers learned to shop on it. In their own words: <em>"The small squares were the deciding point. Think about it a minute - keeps the fill nicely in place."</em> And: <em>"I have another weighted blanket that has large squares and the pressure becomes too much throughout the night."</em></p>

<p>The BareEarth runs a 4"×4" double-stitched grid — inside the band buyers treat as quality, with each small seam carrying a fraction of the load a big baffle puts on its stitching. And yes, we'll say it plainly: <strong>YnM's 2-inch grid is tighter than BareEarth's on paper.</strong> Rank 5 wins the headline number. The catch is what it costs to hold those tiny pockets together — a seven-layer construction that owners consistently describe as sleeping hot, with documented bead leaks at the stressed seams over time. The containment and the heat are the same design decision. BareEarth's grid holds the fill with two fewer problems attached.</p>

