<h3>The second thing — waking up sweating at 3am</h3>

<p>The other way this category loses people: <em>"Weighted blankets are amazing until you're sweating at 3am."</em> The culprit is almost always the fill. Plastic pellets are bulky, and bulky fill needs thick layers around it — the whole package works like insulation. Glass microbeads are dense and small; they carry the same weight in a fraction of the volume and conduct heat away instead of hoarding it. It's why <em>"glass beads vs. plastic pellets — go glass every time"</em> is standing advice wherever owners compare notes.</p>

<p>BareEarth is glass-bead fill in a single-piece build — no pellet bulk, no seven-layer sandwich. The honest limit, printed on the card as con number one: the cover is microfiber, not bamboo or open-knit cotton. It's soft and it washes at home, but if you run genuinely hot and temperature is your single deciding factor, Baloo and Bearaby breathe better — for $198 and $249+ respectively. For everyone else, the glass-and-grid combination is the better trade by about $100.</p>

<h3>"But what weight do I even get?"</h3>

<p>The question that stalls more carts than any other, answered in one line: <strong>about 10% of your body weight.</strong> BareEarth maps it to two options — 15 lb if you weigh 120–170 lb, 18 lb above that. The rule came out of occupational-therapy practice and some OTs go heavier for sensory-seeking users, but here's the practical wisdom from thousands of reviews: too heavy is the mistake people actually make. <em>"I bought one a couple of years ago, but it was too heavy, and unpleasant to use, so I got rid of it right away."</em> Between two weights, take the lighter one. The weight should hold you, not pin you.</p>

